    "note": "A single line is the wrong shape for this subject, and the reader should know it before starting. What follows is one axis drawn through something that has never been one thing. From the second century at the latest there were Christianities in the plural — in Greek, Latin, Syriac, Coptic, Geʽez and Armenian, under Roman, Persian, Arab, Mongol and Ottoman government — and the habit of treating the Mediterranean story as the trunk and everything else as a branch is a habit of the surviving sources rather than a fact about the past. Where this axis has room for one event it has left out five, and nearly every entry below is the visible summit of a submerged range. Dating is the second caution. Very little on this line is fixed by a dated document written at the time before the fourth century — the Delphi inscription that anchors Paul and the sacrifice certificates of the Decian persecution are among the exceptions — and the year at the head of the whole sequence, the crucifixion, is inferred rather than recorded, as are the years in which the gospels were written. Where a church dates something differently from the historians, this timeline gives both accounts and names who holds what, on the principle that a tradition's memory of its own beginning is good evidence about that tradition even where it is not evidence about the year. Several of the most familiar dates also turn out not to be what they are usually taken for: the Edict of Milan was neither an edict nor issued at Milan, the Ninety-Five Theses may never have been nailed to anything, and the schism of 1054 was barely noticed by the people it is supposed to have divided. Nor does everyone count from the same point. The years below are given in the Common Era, which is the reckoning of the Latin West carried outward by its empires; the Copts number from the accession of the emperor who persecuted them and are presently in 1742 of the Martyrs, the Ethiopian calendar runs seven or eight years behind this one, the Armenians have an era of their own beginning in 552, and Byzantium counted from the creation of the world until it stopped counting. The choice of era is a convenience and also, quietly, an argument, and it should be held loosely. The line ends where it does deliberately. It closes not on a division but on the long work of undoing one: the discovery, made slowly across the second half of the twentieth century and still largely unknown outside the commissions that made it, that the fiercest christological quarrel in Christian history had been in substantial part two vocabularies failing, for fifteen hundred years, to hear each other — a finding the theologians have made and the churches have not yet finished receiving.",
