            "end": 33,
            "circa": true,
            "circaEnd": true,
            "description": "A Galilean teacher was executed outside Jerusalem by Roman authority, on the order of the prefect Pontius Pilate, at or about Passover, by a method the empire reserved for slaves and rebels; the charge posted over him, in the gospels' account, named him king of the Jews. Within a very short time — short enough that Paul is quoting a formula he says he received rather than composed — his followers were saying that God had raised him, and were saying it in Jerusalem, where the grave was. Everything on this axis descends from that week and from the claim made about it, and the claim is the one thing the historian cannot adjudicate: that a man was raised is not a statement of the kind evidence settles either way. What can be said is that the movement's earliest surviving documents treat the resurrection not as a doctrine developed later but as the thing they existed to announce.",
            "dating": "Two years are seriously argued, and the argument runs on astronomy. Pilate was prefect of Judaea from 26 to 36 — his name and title survive on a limestone block found at Caesarea in 1961 — and the gospels place the death on a Friday at Passover, which narrows the field to years in which 14 or 15 Nisan fell on a Friday: 7 April 30 and 3 April 33 are the surviving candidates, the Synoptic chronology favouring the fifteenth and John's the fourteenth. Most scholars take 30, a substantial minority 33; Luke's dating of the Baptist's preaching to the fifteenth year of Tiberius pulls one way and John's three Passovers another. The traditional Western reckoning of 33 descends from Eusebius and from Dionysius Exiguus, and the Anno Domini count is itself out by several years, since Matthew and Luke place the birth under Herod the Great, who died in 4 BC. The bare fact of the execution under Pilate is attested outside the Christian sources by Tacitus, writing about 116, and in the Antiquities of Josephus, whose notice of Jesus survives only in a form later Christian copyists have demonstrably touched."
