            "end": 58,
            "circa": true,
            "circaEnd": true,
            "description": "The oldest Christian writings that survive are not gospels but working correspondence: letters dictated by a travelling Jewish apostle to small assemblies he had founded around the Aegean, dealing with a collection for Jerusalem, with a man living with his father's wife, with whether meat from a temple butcher may be eaten, with a runaway slave. They were written to be read aloud in a room, they answer questions we mostly cannot hear, and they were copied and traded between congregations until they were scripture. In them the decisive quarrel of the first generation is fought out in real time — whether gentiles entering this Jewish movement must take on circumcision and the law — and Paul's answer, that they need not, is why the movement did not remain a party within Judaism. Seven are undisputed: Romans, First and Second Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, First Thessalonians and Philemon.",
            "dating": "Pauline chronology hangs on a single stone. An inscription from Delphi, a rescript of the emperor Claudius, fixes the proconsulship of Gallio to about 51 or 52, and Acts brings Paul before Gallio at Corinth; from that anchor the letters are placed, First Thessalonians about 50 and Romans about 56 or 57, with a margin of a year or two either side. The relative order is argued — Galatians is put anywhere from 48 to 55, depending on which visit to Jerusalem it corresponds to — and Philippians and Philemon may come from an Ephesian imprisonment that Acts does not record. Of the six remaining letters under his name, most scholars judge Ephesians, Colossians and Second Thessalonians disputed and the three Pastorals later, written in his name by admirers, a practice that carried no dishonour in the ancient schools. The churches that read them as canonical have generally not accepted that judgement: the Orthodox, Catholic and most evangelical traditions receive all thirteen as Paul's."
