            "description": "The revolt that began in 66 ended with the legions of Titus in Jerusalem and the Temple in flames in the summer of 70; the sacrificial worship of Israel stopped that week and has not resumed. For Judaism the consequence was the long turn towards the synagogue, the study house and the rabbis. For the Christians the consequence was a shift in centre of gravity so complete that it is easy to miss: the Jerusalem church of James, the movement's mother community, ceases to be its centre, and the traditions about Jesus are written into gospels for congregations increasingly gentile and outside Palestine. The Arch of Titus in Rome still shows the menorah being carried away. The separation of church and synagogue is often dated from here, and it should not be — what happens in 70 is not a divorce but the removal of the institution around which both had been arguing.",
            "dating": "The military chronology comes almost entirely from Josephus, who commanded on the Jewish side, changed sides, and wrote for a Flavian audience: the richest source for the period and the least disinterested. He dates the Temple's burning to the tenth of the month Loos, which Jewish tradition remembers as the ninth of Av, the same date it assigns to the destruction of the first Temple. Eusebius reports that the Jerusalem Christians left before the siege for Pella in the Decapolis on the strength of an oracle; the tradition is early, unverifiable, and has been both defended and dismissed. The larger question — when Judaism and Christianity became two religions rather than one argument — has moved a long way from the older model of a clean parting after 70. Work since the 1990s, Daniel Boyarin's and the Becker and Reed collection of 2003 above all, argues for overlapping communities into the fourth century and later, and for a separation produced in part by bishops and rabbis insisting on one where ordinary people saw none."
