            "end": null,
            "circa": true,
            "circaEnd": null,
            "description": "A bishop of Antioch, under guard and travelling west to be killed in the Roman arena, wrote seven letters on the road — to Ephesus, Magnesia, Tralles, Rome, Philadelphia, Smyrna, and to Polycarp — and they are unlike anything else surviving from the period: urgent, physical, and wholly unafraid. He begs the Roman congregation not to intervene to save him, saying he is God's wheat and wishes to be ground by the teeth of the beasts. He insists that a congregation do nothing without its bishop, and he is the first writer anywhere to use the phrase the catholic church. Two things that would matter enormously afterwards are already fully formed in him: the single bishop as the visible centre of the local church, and the conviction that martyrdom is not a disaster to be endured but a communion to be attained.",
            "dating": "The letters exist in three recensions — a short Syriac, a middle Greek, and a long interpolated one — and the seventeenth century spent itself deciding which was authentic. James Ussher in 1644 and John Pearson after him argued for the middle recension, and J. B. Lightfoot's edition of 1885 settled the matter for most scholars; a Latin translation made by Robert Grosseteste in the thirteenth century turned out to preserve the middle text. On the traditional dating, which comes from Eusebius, Ignatius died under Trajan, between 98 and 117, and about 107 or 110 is usually given. That dating has been under sustained attack since the 1970s: Robert Joly, Reinhard Hübner, Thomas Lechner and, in 2008, Timothy Barnes have argued that the monarchical episcopate the letters assume did not exist so early and that the corpus belongs to the 140s or later, perhaps to the circle around Polycarp. The majority still holds the traditional date; the challenge has not been answered so much as absorbed as a permanent uncertainty."
