            "description": "A shipowner from Sinope on the Black Sea, wealthy, generous to the Roman church and eventually expelled from it, Marcion concluded that the God who made this world and gave the law was not the Father of Jesus Christ: a just god, not an evil one, but not the good and hitherto unknown God whom Christ revealed. He therefore discarded the Hebrew scriptures entirely and issued instead a Gospel — a version of Luke — and ten letters of Paul, edited of what he took to be Judaising corruption, prefaced by a book of Antitheses setting the two testaments against each other. His churches spread across the empire and lasted for centuries. He is the reason the question of what belongs in the book became urgent, and the answer the wider church gave — that the God of Israel is the Father of Jesus, and that the scriptures of Israel are Christian scripture — is among the most consequential decisions it ever took.",
            "dating": "Tertullian, writing against him about 208, dates the break with the Roman church a hundred and fifteen years and six months after the fifteenth year of Tiberius — not after the crucifixion, as the figure is often loosely reported — and that reckoning is where the conventional date of about 144 comes from; Marcion's birth and death are unknown, and every line of his own writing is lost. Everything we have comes from opponents — Justin, Irenaeus, Tertullian, Epiphanius, the Syriac Ephrem — who quote in order to refute, so that reconstructing his Gospel means reading five hostile witnesses against each other. Adolf von Harnack's Marcion of 1921 made him the founder of the Christian canon and has shaped a century of discussion. Since about 2015 a serious minority — Matthias Klinghardt, Markus Vinzent, Jason BeDuhn in a more qualified way — has argued that his Gospel is not an abridgement of Luke but an earlier text that Luke expanded, which if sustained would reorder the whole synoptic question. Most specialists are unpersuaded, and the debate is live."
