            "description": "In a Phrygian village a man named Montanus began to prophesy in ecstasy, speaking in the first person as the Paraclete, and two women, Priscilla and Maximilla, prophesied with him. They called what they had the New Prophecy, kept rigorous fasts, refused second marriage, expected the heavenly Jerusalem to descend at Pepuza, and held that grave sin after baptism was beyond the church's power to forgive. Their opponents called them the Phrygians, or after the founder, so that the name Montanism is their enemies' rather than their own. The quarrel it forced was not doctrinal — nobody accused them of getting the Trinity wrong — but institutional: whether the Spirit may still speak in a new voice after the apostles, and who decides. The bishops decided that it may not, and the church that emerged from the argument was more ordered, more textual and less immediately prophetic than the one that went into it. Tertullian, the fiercest mind Latin Christianity had yet produced, joined them.",
            "dating": "The two ancient dates conflict and neither can be confidently preferred: Eusebius, following an anonymous opponent, places the outbreak in 172; Epiphanius gives 157. Modern discussion generally allows a range from about 155 to about 175. The movement is far better attested in its opponents than in itself — a handful of prophetic oracles survive as quotations and the rest is polemic, though inscriptions from Phrygia have added evidence the literary sources do not supply. Its life was very long: condemned in Asia in the second century, it persisted into the sixth, and John of Ephesus reports the destruction of the shrine at Pepuza under Justinian. The word Montanist is an opponents' coinage and is used here only because no other has survived. When the group named itself, it said the New Prophecy."
