            "circa": false,
            "circaEnd": false,
            "description": "Persecution before the middle of the third century was local, sporadic and usually provoked from below. In 250 the emperor Decius changed that by requiring every inhabitant of the empire to sacrifice and to obtain a certificate saying so; about forty-five of those certificates survive on papyrus from Egypt, ordinary documents witnessing an extraordinary demand. Valerian followed in 257, and then in 303 Diocletian issued the edicts of the Great Persecution: churches demolished, scriptures surrendered, clergy imprisoned, and finally universal sacrifice. What the persecutions produced was not only martyrs but a problem the church has never entirely put down — what to do with those who complied. The lapsed, the certificate-buyers, and above all the bishops who handed over the books were the occasion of the Novatianist schism at Rome, of Cyprian's whole work at Carthage, and of the Donatist division in North Africa that outlasted the Western empire itself.",
            "dating": "The Decian edict of 249 or 250 does not survive; it is reconstructed from the certificates and from Cyprian, and whether it was aimed at Christians specifically or was a general act of imperial piety that caught them is argued, with recent work favouring the latter. The Great Persecution's first edict is dated 24 February 303, and its course was wildly uneven: effectively over in the West by 306, it continued in the East under Galerius and then Maximinus Daia past Galerius's edict of toleration of 30 April 311, and ceased only with Maximinus's defeat and death in 313 — which is where this span closes, rather than at 311. Martyr numbers are unrecoverable. Eusebius's figures are impressionistic and the acts of the martyrs are a literary genre as much as a record; Candida Moss's argument of 2013 that the scale was systematically magnified in later Christian memory has been influential and heavily contested. The Coptic Orthodox Church dates its calendar from Diocletian's accession in 284 and reckons the present year 1742 of the Martyrs — counting, that is, from the persecutor."
