            "description": "While the persecutions were still in living memory, Egyptian Christians began walking out of the villages into the desert, and within two generations there were thousands of them: solitaries at Nitria and Scetis, the ordered communities Pachomius organised at Tabennesi, the cells of the Judaean wilderness and the harsher improvisations of Syria. Their sayings were collected — the Apophthegmata Patrum, in which an abba or an amma answers a visitor in a sentence and declines to explain it — and the women among them, Syncletica, Sarah, Theodora, stand in the collection under their own names. This is the beginning of monasticism and also of something larger: an alternative to the martyr's witness now that the arena had closed, a school of attention and self-knowledge whose vocabulary the whole later tradition borrowed, and, in Athanasius's Life of Antony, a book that turned an unlettered Copt into one of the most widely read figures in Latin Europe.",
            "dating": "Antony's withdrawal is placed about 270 on the strength of the Life, which gives him a hundred and five years and a death on 17 January 356; Pachomius's foundation at Tabennesi is set about 323 and his death in 346. That Antony was the first is the Life's claim rather than a fact — it concedes an older ascetic nearby, Jerome awarded the priority to Paul of Thebes, and the papyri show anchorites in Egypt before him. The closing date of about 400 marks not an ending but the point at which the movement is established from Gaul to Mesopotamia; monastic Christianity is still going on. The Apophthegmata were collected in Greek in the fifth and sixth centuries out of material that had circulated in Coptic, so the desert's own language reaches us mostly in translation, and Samuel Rubenson's study of Antony's Letters found behind the illiterate hero of the Life a theologian at home in Origen. Which Antony is the historical one is not agreed."
