            "description": "On 28 October 312 Constantine defeated Maxentius at the Milvian Bridge north of Rome and became master of the West; the following February he and Licinius met at Milan and agreed that Christians and everyone else should be free to follow the worship they chose, and that confiscated church property should be restored. Within a decade the emperor was building basilicas, exempting clergy from civic burdens, and summoning bishops to settle their own quarrels at public expense. Nothing on this axis altered the daily conditions of Christian life more. A movement that had been illegal, socially suspect and occasionally hunted became first tolerated, then favoured, then — by the end of the century — the religion of the state, acquiring in the process the machinery of empire, the temptations of patronage, and the habit of settling doctrine in assemblies convened by a layman who held no office in the church at all.",
            "dating": "The so-called Edict of Milan was neither an edict nor promulgated at Milan: what survives, in Lactantius's Latin and in Eusebius's Greek, is a letter to provincial governors issued by Licinius at Nicomedia in June 313, giving effect to an agreement the two emperors had reached at Milan in February. Nor was it the first act of toleration — Galerius had ended the persecution by edict on 30 April 311. Constantine's vision is reported twice and differently: Lactantius, writing within a few years, has a dream the night before the battle instructing him to mark his soldiers' shields; Eusebius, writing much later and saying the emperor swore to it, has a cross of light in the sky at midday with the words conquer by this. The two accounts cannot be harmonised; the sincerity and content of Constantine's belief have been argued since Burckhardt; and he was baptised only on his deathbed in 337, which was ordinary practice at the time and is not evidence either way."
