            "description": "Constantine summoned the bishops of the empire to Nicaea in Bithynia to settle a quarrel that had begun in Alexandria: Arius, a presbyter of that city, taught that the Son was the first and greatest of all creatures, brought into being by the Father's will, so that there was when he was not. The council answered with a creed declaring the Son begotten not made, of one substance — homoousios — with the Father, and anathematising Arius's formulations. It also fixed a common reckoning for Easter and issued twenty canons of ordinary church discipline. What Nicaea did not do is worth stating plainly, because it is so often said otherwise: it did not decide which books belong in the Bible, it did not invent the divinity of Christ, and its decision was not close. What it did do was hand the church a word not found in scripture, and commit it to the consequences.",
            "dating": "The council sat from May to about the end of July 325 and its acts do not survive; we have the creed, the canons, a synodal letter, and the reports of participants, chiefly Eusebius of Caesarea and, later and polemically, Athanasius. The traditional number of three hundred and eighteen fathers is first attested in the middle of the fourth century and is a symbolic figure taken from the servants of Abraham in Genesis; contemporary estimates run from about two hundred and fifty to three hundred. Nicaea settled nothing for two generations: Arius was recalled, Athanasius exiled five times, and much of the fourth-century episcopate held positions between the two poles which the winning side afterwards flattened into a single label. Arianism as a coherent party is largely a construction of Athanasian polemic — the point argued by Maurice Wiles, Rowan Williams and Lewis Ayres — and the churches that later evangelised the Goths and Vandals, called Arian ever since, would not have recognised the name."
