            "year": 431,
            "end": null,
            "circa": false,
            "circaEnd": null,
            "description": "Nestorius, a Syrian monk made archbishop of Constantinople, objected to calling Mary Theotokos, God-bearer, on the ground that a creature cannot be the source of divinity, and proposed Christotokos instead; Cyril of Alexandria heard in this a Christ divided into two subjects, a man in whom God dwelt rather than God made man. The council Cyril opened at Ephesus before the Syrian bishops had arrived deposed Nestorius and affirmed Theotokos. Two years later Cyril and John of Antioch signed a Formula of Reunion conceding much of what the Antiochenes had wanted, but the deposition stood, and the Church of the East — the great church of the Persian empire and later of Central Asia, China and India — would not accept it, holding to its teachers Diodore and Theodore and honouring Nestorius as a man wronged. That church has been called Nestorian by everybody else ever since. It rejects the name.",
            "dating": "The council opened on 22 June 431 and its proceedings were disorderly even by conciliar standards: Cyril presided over his own case, the eastern bishops arrived to find it decided and held a rival council deposing him, and the emperor for a time had both men under arrest. Nestorius's own theology is much harder to establish than the label suggests, and the recovery in a Syriac version of his Book of Heracleides, published in 1910, showed a position closer to what Chalcedon would later define than his condemnation implies; whether he was a Nestorian in the textbook sense is now seriously doubted. The Assyrian Church of the East holds that it never taught two persons in Christ and that the condemnation turned on the untranslatability of Greek terms into Syriac; the Common Christological Declaration of 1994 records Rome and that church confessing the same faith in Christ. The Coptic, Syriac, Armenian and Ethiopian churches receive Ephesus as the third ecumenical council; the Assyrian Church of the East does not."
