            "circaEnd": null,
            "description": "The council that met across the water from Constantinople in the autumn of 451 defined Christ as acknowledged in two natures, without confusion, without change, without division, without separation, in one person and one hypostasis; it deposed Dioscorus of Alexandria, condemned Eutyches, who had taught that after the union there was only one nature, and endorsed the Tome of Leo. It is the deepest and most durable division in Christian history — deeper than 1054, older than 1517, and unhealed. Egypt, most of Syria, Armenia and later Ethiopia would not accept the formula, holding with Cyril to one incarnate nature of God the Word, out of two: miaphysis, not monophysis, a distinction the churches concerned insist upon and their opponents ignored for fifteen centuries. From 451 there are Christianities in the plural as an institutional fact, and every subsequent map of the faith is drawn over this line.",
            "dating": "The council sat from 8 October to 1 November 451; its acts are unusually full and were edited early. The Oriental Orthodox note, and the acts bear them out, that Dioscorus was sentenced on canonical grounds — chiefly his refusal to appear when summoned three times — and not by a doctrinal condemnation. The date is exact and the division it names is not an event but a process: rival Alexandrian successions before and after Zeno's Henotikon of 482, the re-imposition of Chalcedon by Justin I in 518, and only with Jacob Baradaeus's ordination of a parallel hierarchy from the 540s do two permanent communions exist. The Armenian Church, whose bishops were absent in 451 fighting the Persians at Avarayr, rejected the definition at Dvin in the following century. Monophysite is a polemical exonym rejected by the Oriental Orthodox churches, which anathematise Eutyches as firmly as Chalcedon did; the term they use is miaphysite. Whether the quarrel was ever substantive, or a collision of Greek, Syriac and Coptic vocabularies aggravated by imperial politics, is argued in every generation — and the agreed statements of 1989 and 1990 concluded, on behalf of both families, that the faith confessed is the same."
